1 NOTE {geni:about_me} GARZA FALCÓN, JOSÉ ANTONIO DE LA (1739–1797). José Antonio de la Garza Falcón, soldier and public official, was born in 1739 in Cerralvo, Nuevo León, Mexico, the son of Capt. Blas María de la Garza Falcón and Catar
2 CONC ina Gómez de Castro. He received his education in Cerralvo and Camargo, joined the Spanish presidial company of Camargo, and later became a lieutenant in the Cavalry Militia of Province and Frontier stationed at Camargo. In 1766 r
2 CONC umors that the English were preparing to establish themselves on the lower Texas coast led to several requests that Garza Falcón investigate the scene. In the summer of 1776, responding to a request from José de Escandón, who hims
2 CONC elf was acting at the behest of the viceroy, the Marqués de Cruillasqv, Garza Falcón explored the coast from the Rio Grande to Corpus Christi Bay. Later that year he was ordered to explore Padre Island by Diego Ortiz Parrilla,qqv 
2 CONC who had been instructed to investigate the islands off the lower Texas coast. Garza Falcón may have complied, or he may simply have given Ortiz Parrilla a report, dated September 1766, that was based on his exploration several mon
2 CONC ths earlier. One historian believes that the latter case is more likely, because high water from a hurricane earlier in September would have prevented him and his group from crossing over to the island. In addition, Garza's descri
2 CONC ption of the island makes no mention of the effects of the storm. The name Corpus Christi Bay was apparently bestowed by either Garza Falcón or by his father, who owned the nearby Santa Petronila Ranch. After his father died in 17
2 CONC 67, Garza Falcón became captain and chief justice of Camargo and its jurisdiction, which extended to Matamoros, Tamaulipas, and to the Nueces River in Texas. He was appointed in 1767 to assign Spanish land grants north and south o
2 CONC f the Rio Grande to 111 settlers from Camargo. He married Leonor Mendez, daughter of Bartolomé Mendez and Francisca Gonzales, on April 12, 1773, in Camargo. Two daughters were born to them, one of whom, Guadalupe, married alcalde 
2 CONC and lieutenant Blas de la Garza (1772–1820) at Matamoros. After his death, she married Lt. Felipe Roque de la Portilla. Garza Falcón died in Camargo in 1797 and was buried in the private family chapel, Nuestra Señora de Guadalupe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Citation: Clotilde P. García, "GARZA FALCON, JOSE ANTONIO DE LA," Handbook of Texas Online (http://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/online/articles/fgaaf), accessed April 21, 2014. Uploaded on June 15, 2010. Published by the Texas Stat
2 CONC e Historical Association
